+# Retention planning: summary
+
+## What changed
+
+- `vaultsnap/retention.py`: implemented `plan_retention(snapshots, policy, now)`. It only plans; nothing is deleted. `Plan` and the signature are unchanged.
+- `tests/test_retention_rules.py` (new): 31 tests for the ADR rules that the existing `tests/test_retention.py` does not cover. The existing file is unchanged.
+- No other files were changed. `docs/adr/README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` and `README.md` were not touched (see "Stale docs" below).
+
+## Which records are in force
+
+The index `docs/adr/README.md` is out of date: it stops at ADR-0007, and each record's own Status line governs. Read per section:
+
+- **ADR-0001** (Accepted, amended by section): applies, except:
+  - "Weeks" is replaced by ADR-0003.
+  - "Day boundaries" defers to ADR-0002, which is superseded, so the rule comes from ADR-0004.
+  - The rule count and the empty-policy sentence are amended by ADR-0006 and ADR-0008.
+  - The monthly row of the bucket table is amended by ADR-0010.
+  - "Set-aside" and "Output" are extended by ADR-0007, ADR-0008 and ADR-0009.
+- **ADR-0002** (UTC buckets): **not in force**, superseded by ADR-0004.
+- **ADR-0003** (ISO weeks): in force. Weekly key is `isocalendar()[:2]`, Monday start.
+- **ADR-0004** (`tz_offset_minutes`, `day_start_hour`): in force, except the daylight-saving sentence, which ADR-0005 supersedes.
+- **ADR-0005** (`zone`: `lakeside`, `harbor`): in force. Offset comes from the row table at the instant `taken_at`; `tz_offset_minutes` is then ignored.
+- **ADR-0006** (`keep_within_hours`): in force for the window definition and the six-rule count. Its "Interaction with the bucket rules" section is **not in force**, reverted by ADR-0009.
+- **ADR-0007** (parents): in force. Validation errors and the held-parent step.
+- **ADR-0008** (`expire_tags`, order of overrides): in force.
+- **ADR-0009** (window set aside): in force. Adds the window as line 4 of the override order.
+- **ADR-0010** (`month_start_day`): in force, for the monthly key only.
+
+## Which record decides which part
+
+| Part | Records |
+|---|---|
+| Input validation: naive datetimes, duplicate ids, unknown parent, parent cycles (any policy) | 0001 "Inputs", 0007 |
+| Ordering: later `taken_at` is newer, ties go to the greater id | 0001 "Ordering" |
+| Override order: protected, future, expired, window, remaining | 0001 "Set-aside", 0008, 0009 |
+| Protected and future snapshots are kept and set aside | 0001, 0008 |
+| Expired snapshots are pruned and excluded from rules | 0008 |
+| Window (`now - taken_at <= hours`, `None`/`0` off) is kept and set aside | 0006, 0009 |
+| `keep_last`, and the daily, weekly, monthly and yearly rules over remaining snapshots | 0001, 0003, 0004, 0005, 0010 |
+| Bucket date: convert to offset (fixed or zone), subtract `day_start_hour`, take the date | 0004, 0005 |
+| Zone offset lookup (latest row not later than the instant; initial offset before the first row) | 0005 |
+| Monthly cycle key with `month_start_day` | 0010 |
+| Dependency step: kept snapshots hold parents, transitively; runs last and can override expiry | 0007, 0008 |
+| Empty policy (no rule on): every remaining snapshot is kept | 0001, 0006, 0008 |
+
+## Interpretations
+
+- A rule is "on" when its value is non-zero and not `None`. `keep_within_hours` counts as one of the six rules. So a policy whose only rule is the window keeps the window and prunes everything older.
+- A snapshot exactly at `now` is not future. It is set aside only if the window is on.
+- A future snapshot that is also expired is kept (line 2 is above line 3).
+- `plan_retention` also raises `ValueError` if `taken_at` is not a datetime at all. The ADRs only mention naive datetimes.
+- Validation runs before any planning and raises under every policy.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `python -m unittest` before the change: 7 errors, all `NotImplementedError`.
+- `python -m unittest` after the change: `Ran 38 tests ... OK` (the 7 existing tests plus 31 new ones).
+- Three of my new tests failed on the first run. All three had the same cause in the test setup, not the code: the clock was 2026-03-20, so the 2027–2028 snapshots (and the 2026-03-29 zone snapshots) were in the future and were correctly kept as set aside. I moved the test clock to 2030-01-01 and did not change the code. I did not edit the existing tests.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- The zone tables are taken from ADR-0005 as written. I did not check them against any real time zone data, and the ADR says it makes no real-world claim.
+- I did not run this against real snapshot data, and there is no end-to-end test of pruning. `plan_retention` is pure, so nothing is deleted by this change.
+- I did not check the code for performance on large snapshot lists. It sorts once and runs one pass per rule.
+- The new test file uses a fixed clock (2030-01-01) so that its dates are never in the future. This is a test choice, not part of the spec.
+- Python 3.14.3 is the only interpreter I ran against.
+
+## Stale docs (outside the allowed files, not changed)
+
+- `docs/adr/README.md`: the index lists ADR-0001 to 0007 only, and the 0002 status is wrong for the current state. It needs rows for 0008 to 0010 and the sections noted above.
+- `README.md`: the retention section is the old draft and should describe the ADRs or point to them.
+- `CHANGELOG.md`: its "Unreleased" entry says `plan_retention` "implements them", which is true now. I did not review the rest of the file against the ADRs.
